---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dividerCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'r1', label: 'R1', min: 100, max: 10000, step: 100, value: 1000, unit: 'Ω' },
    { name: 'r2', label: 'R2', min: 100, max: 10000, step: 100, value: 2000, unit: 'Ω' },
    { name: 'vin', label: 'Vin', min: 1, max: 24, step: 0.5, value: 9, unit: 'V' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'Vout = Vin·R2/(R1+R2)', expr: 'vin*r2/(r1+r2)', unit: 'V' },
    { label: 'current', expr: 'vin/(r1+r2)', unit: 'A' },
  ],
};

const lawsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Ohm\'s law', note: 'V = IR. Voltage is the push, current the flow, resistance the friction. Three symbols carry an entire discipline.' },
    { math: '2 · Kirchhoff\'s current law', note: 'Charge does not accumulate at a node: current in = current out. That is conservation of charge, written for a junction.' },
    { math: '3 · Kirchhoff\'s voltage law', note: 'Around any closed loop the voltage rises and drops sum to zero — conservation of energy for a circuit walk.' },
    { math: '4 · Series and parallel simplify', note: 'Series resistances add; parallel conductances add. Most intimidating schematics collapse into these two moves.' },
    { math: '5 · Equivalent sources finish it', note: 'Any linear network seen from two terminals is just one source and one resistor — Thevenin and Norton\'s great compression.' },
  ],
};

const dcQuantityMatch = {
  "prompt": "Three quantities, three physical meanings — pair them:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Voltage",
      "right": "energy per unit charge — joules per coulomb"
    },
    {
      "left": "Current",
      "right": "the flow of charge itself — coulombs per second"
    },
    {
      "left": "Resistance",
      "right": "the friction between them — push needed to sustain a given flow"
    },
    {
      "left": "the water analogy",
      "right": "pressure across a pipe run, litres per second, pipe narrowness"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Hold the analogy loosely — electrons do not pour out of wires and drain away, charge cannot spill or splash, and the analogy says nothing about fields. Climb the ladder, then leave it behind."
};

const dcOhmTrace = {
  "prompt": "V–I lines for three resistors (voltage up, current across). Which line is the 200 Ω resistor?",
  "curves": [
    {
      "expr": "100*x",
      "color": "#1d4ed8",
      "label": "line P"
    },
    {
      "expr": "200*x",
      "color": "#b42318",
      "label": "line Q"
    },
    {
      "expr": "50*x",
      "color": "#0f766e",
      "label": "line R"
    }
  ],
  "correct": 1,
  "x": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 0.06
  },
  "y": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 12
  },
  "xlabel": "current I (A)",
  "ylabel": "voltage V (V)",
  "why": "Resistance is the slope: at 60 mA the 200 Ω line asks 12 V, the 100 Ω asks 6 V, the 50 Ω asks 3 V — \"double the push, double the flow\" reads straight off the graph."
};

const dcOhmBuckets = {
  "prompt": "V = IR is a property, not a conservation law. File each case:",
  "buckets": [
    "Ohm's law holds",
    "Ohm's law breaks"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "a metal wire at constant temperature",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "a diode",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a lightbulb filament as it heats",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "a metal at a steady cool operating temperature",
      "bucket": 0
    }
  ],
  "why": "A diode ignores the law outright and a heating filament drifts out of line — yet Kirchhoff's laws keep working in circuits where Ohm's law fails everywhere. KCL and KVL are the two pillars; Ohm is the convenient helper."
};

const dcSolveOrder = {
  "prompt": "12 V into a 100 Ω series resistor and two 200 Ω in parallel — put the solve in order:",
  "items": [
    "Collapse the parallel pair: 200 ÷ 2 = 100 Ω, so the total is 200 Ω",
    "Ohm's law gives the battery current: 12 ÷ 200 = 60 mA",
    "KVL splits the volts: 0.06 × 100 = 6 V, leaving 6 V across the pair",
    "KCL finishes: 6 V across each 200 Ω branch drives 30 mA per branch",
    "Check: 30 + 30 = 60 mA — balanced at every node"
  ],
  "why": "Ohm converts push into flow, KVL constrains voltages, KCL constrains currents — the same pattern you will later automate with nodal analysis."
};

const dcDividerSpot = {
  "prompt": "A pupil derives the divider formula. One line is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "One current flows through both resistors: I = Vin ÷ (R1 + R2)",
    "The output is taken across R2, so Vout = I × R2",
    "Substituting: Vout = Vin × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2)",
    "Check with the defaults — 9 V, R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 2 kΩ: Vout = 3 V"
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Vout = 9 × 2 ÷ (1 + 2) = 6 V — the tap takes the fraction 2 ÷ 3 of the input.",
  "why": "The current readout changes with the sum of the resistors, but the output fraction depends on R2 ÷ (R1 + R2) — double R2 and the fraction rises; double R1 and it falls."
};

const dcPowerCloze = {
  "text": "9 V across 3 kΩ drives %%0%% mA, so the battery delivers %%1%% mW. The 1 kΩ resistor, dropping 3 V, dissipates %%2%% mW; the 2 kΩ, dropping 6 V, takes the other %%3%% mW — and the books balance because KVL is %%4%% wearing a circuit's clothes.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "3"
    ],
    [
      "27"
    ],
    [
      "9"
    ],
    [
      "18"
    ],
    [
      "conservation of energy"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "P = VI = I²R = V²/R — pick the form that matches the question."
};

const dcLoadPredict = {
  "q": "The unloaded tap reads 6 V. Close the load onto it — what does the meter read now?",
  "choices": [
    "Still 6 V — the tap holds",
    "About 4.5 V",
    "About 3.6 V",
    "About 0.6 V"
  ],
  "answer": 2,
  "why": "6 × 1000 ÷ 1667 ≈ 3.6 V — a 40 percent sag the naive divider formula never predicted. The cure: divider resistances small compared with the load, shrinking the Thevenin resistance."
};

const dcTransferEstimate = {
  "prompt": "A load extracts the most power from a Thevenin source when its resistance equals the Thevenin resistance. What share of the generated power reaches the load at that match point?",
  "unit": "% efficiency",
  "answer": 50,
  "factor": 1.5,
  "why": "Half — which is fine for a radio antenna and deliberately avoided in a power grid, where matching the generator would waste half the plant.",
  "context": "Maximum power and maximum voltage transfer are different goals: deliver energy and you avoid the match; deliver information and you design for it."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/electrical-engineering/foundational/dc-circuit-analysis">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Electrical engineering. You need nothing from earlier
    lessons — only multiplication, division, and the patience to label things carefully
    before computing. Everything else is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define voltage, current and resistance precisely, and say what each one physically measures;</li>
    <li>apply Ohm's law and Kirchhoff's two laws, and explain which conservation principle each expresses;</li>
    <li>simplify series and parallel networks — and recognise the circuits where this fails;</li>
    <li>solve any resistor network with nodal or mesh analysis, and explain why SPICE does the same thing;</li>
    <li>reduce any linear network to its Thevenin or Norton equivalent, and predict loading effects;</li>
    <li>compute power dissipation and explain why transmission grids run at enormous voltages.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: before touching any algebra, <em>name the nodes and mark the reference
    directions</em>. Most circuit errors are not arithmetic failures; they are sign and
    bookkeeping failures — and the professionals' fix is a drawing discipline, not a
    better calculator.
  </p>

  <h2>The friendliest system in all of engineering</h2>
  <p>
    A circuit is one of the friendliest systems you will ever analyse: a closed world
    where exactly two quantities matter and exactly two conservation laws govern them.
    <strong>Voltage</strong> is electrical "height" — energy per unit charge, measured in
    joules per coulomb. <strong>Current</strong> is the flow of charge itself, coulombs
    per second, measured in amperes. <strong>Resistance</strong> is the friction between
    them — how much push is needed to sustain a given flow, measured in ohms. The water
    analogy is genuinely useful here: voltage is the pressure difference across a pipe
    run, current is the litres per second moving through it, and resistance is how narrow
    the pipe is. But hold the analogy loosely — electricity is not water. Electrons do not
    pour out of wires and drain away, charge cannot spill or splash, and the analogy says
    nothing about fields. It is a ladder for intuition, to be climbed and then left
    behind.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="pair each quantity with what it physically measures" widget="match" config={dcQuantityMatch} />
  <p>
    What makes circuit analysis remarkable is its completeness. Three laws — Ohm's law
    plus Kirchhoff's two conservation statements — are sufficient to solve <em>any</em>
    network of batteries and resistors, no matter how tangled, to exact algebraic
    precision. There is no statistical approximation, no empirical fudge: the schematic
    on a first-year problem set and the power-distribution network of a jetliner yield to
    the same three sentences. The skills this lesson builds — read the circuit, name the
    nodes, trust conservation — are the same ones a chip designer uses with a thousand
    nodes, except that the thousand-node version is done by software that automates
    precisely the recipe taught here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Direct current (DC) — steady flows, steady voltages — is where the reflexes are
    built. Alternating current adds capacitors, inductors and calculus on top; DC first
    ensures that when that complexity arrives, the conservation backbone underneath it is
    already automatic.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ohm, Kirchhoff, and the network collapses" widget="equationStep" config={lawsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five panels are the whole course in order, and
    each one is a <em>move</em>, not just a fact. Panel 1 converts local push into local
    flow. Panels 2 and 3 are the two conservation laws — one for charge at junctions, one
    for energy around loops. Panel 4 is the shortcut: series and parallel reductions
    collapse most schematics to a single resistor. Panel 5 is the finisher: even the
    circuits that refuse to collapse surrender to one source and one resistor. Every
    worked example below is one of these five moves — watch which.
  </p>

  <h2>Ohm's law and Kirchhoff's two laws</h2>
  <p>
    Ohm's law, V = IR, states that the current through a resistor is proportional to the
    voltage across it, with the resistance as the constant of proportionality. Double the
    push, double the flow. Kirchhoff's current law (KCL) says the current flowing into
    any junction equals the current flowing out — conservation of charge: charge has
    nowhere to be created or stored at a connection point, so whatever arrives must
    leave. Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL) says the sum of voltage rises and drops around
    any closed loop is zero — conservation of energy: walk a full circuit of the loop and
    you must return to your starting potential, just as a hiker returning to base camp
    has zero net elevation change. Battery rises pay for resistor drops, exactly, every
    time. Note the division of labour: Ohm converts local voltage to local current, KVL
    constrains the voltages, KCL constrains the currents.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="three V–I lines — which one is the 200 Ω part?" widget="trace" config={dcOhmTrace} />
  <p>
    Two bookkeeping conventions keep the algebra honest. A <strong>node</strong> is any
    point where devices connect; one node is chosen as the <strong>reference</strong>
    (called <em>ground</em>, zero volts) and every other voltage is measured against it —
    which saves you from specifying plus and minus pairs for every measurement. And
    current arrows on a diagram are <em>reference directions</em>, not predictions: if
    the true flow turns out opposite, the arithmetic simply returns a negative number,
    which is information, not error.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> current gets "used up" as it goes around a circuit.
  It does not — current is a flow rate of charge, and charge is conserved, so the same
  current that leaves the battery returns to it. What is consumed is <em>energy</em>:
  each coulomb delivers its cargo of joules to the resistors on the way around, emerging
  at lower potential. The river's flow rate is unchanged along its length even as its
  height drops over every waterfall.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the current arrow shows electrons marching that way.
  Two corrections at once. First, conventional current is defined to flow from plus to
  minus — opposite to the electron drift — because the convention predates the
  electron's discovery by a century and works perfectly; think in terms of current and
  never electrons. Second, electrons barely move at all: their drift through a household
  wire runs at a fraction of a millimetre per second, while the electrical effect travels
  near the speed of light. The wave carries the energy; the electrons are the medium,
  jostling their neighbours, not sprinting themselves.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> Ohm's law is a fundamental law of physics, like
  conservation of energy. It is not. It is a material <em>property</em> — a description
  of how metals at constant temperature happen to behave. A diode ignores it outright, a
  lightbulb filament changes its resistance as it heats, and Kirchhoff's laws keep
  working in circuits where Ohm's law fails everywhere. That is why KCL and KVL, not
  Ohm's law, are the two pillars the whole subject rests on.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file the components: does Ohm's law hold?" widget="sort-buckets" config={dcOhmBuckets} />

  <h2>A first solve: the three laws cooperating</h2>
  <p>
    Take a 12 V battery feeding a 100 Ω resistor in series with two 200 Ω resistors that
    are in parallel with each other. The parallel pair has resistance 200 ÷ 2 = 100 Ω
    (two equal resistors split any current equally), so the total is 200 Ω and Ohm's law
    gives a battery current of 12 ÷ 200 = 60 mA. KVL then splits the voltage: the series
    resistor drops 0.06 × 100 = 6 V, leaving 6 V across the pair. KCL finishes the job:
    6 V across each 200 Ω branch drives 30 mA per branch, and indeed 30 + 30 = 60 mA —
    the books balance at every node. Small enough to check by hand, and exactly the
    pattern you will automate later.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="replay the three-law solve in sequence" widget="order" config={dcSolveOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three sentences — V = IR, current balances at every node,
    voltage balances around every loop — plus two bookkeeping conventions (ground and
    reference arrows) already solve any series-parallel circuit. Next, the two
    reductions that spare you the algebra most of the time.
  </p>

  <h2>The voltage divider: the circuit everyone owns</h2>
  <p>
    Two resistors in series split a source voltage in proportion to their resistances:
    Vout = Vin × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2). The derivation is two lines — one current through both
    resistors, KVL for the split — and the result is arguably the most-used circuit in
    existence. Sensors use it to turn resistance changes into voltage changes (a
    thermistor in one half makes a thermometer); microcontrollers use it to scale a
    battery's 4.2 V down to the 3.3 V an analogue input can read; volume controls in the
    analogue era were literally one adjustable divider.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a two-resistor divider under your fingers" widget="sliderLab" config={dividerCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> with the default values — 9 V in, R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 =
    2 kΩ — the output reads 6 V: the tap takes the fraction R2 ÷ (R1 + R2) = 2 ÷ 3 of the
    input. Double R2 and the fraction rises; double R1 instead and it falls. The current
    readout changes with the <em>sum</em> of the resistors — the same current flows
    through both, which is the whole reason the split is proportional. Then note what the
    widget does <em>not</em> model: the moment a load draws current from the tap, the
    formula stops being exact and the output sags — a preview of Thevenin's insight
    below.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="one line of the derivation is wrong — find it" widget="spot-error" config={dcDividerSpot} />

  <h2>Power: where the energy actually goes</h2>
  <p>
    Voltage and current together fix the rate of energy delivery: power equals voltage
    times current, P = VI. Substitute Ohm's law and two more forms appear — P = I²R and
    P = V²/R — which answer the two questions engineers actually ask: how hot will this
    resistor get, and how much current does this device really draw? Run the divider's
    default values through the arithmetic: 9 V across 3 kΩ drives 3 mA, so the battery
    delivers 27 mW. The 1 kΩ resistor, dropping 3 V, dissipates 9 mW; the 2 kΩ, dropping
    6 V, takes the other 18 mW. The books balance — 27 in, 27 out — because KVL is
    conservation of energy wearing a circuit's clothes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the power ledger — milliwatts in, milliwatts out" widget="cloze" config={dcPowerCloze} />
  <p>
    Those milliwatts explain a component you have seen and never questioned: the power
    rating printed on every resistor. A standard part survives a quarter watt; push 0.5 W
    through it and it cooks, darkens and drifts out of specification. The same arithmetic
    scaled up governs real infrastructure — a transmission line carrying megawatts loses
    I²R power as heat, which is precisely why grids transmit at enormous voltages: raising
    voltage cuts current in proportion, and the losses fall with the <em>square</em> of
    current. One line of algebra, P = I²R, is the reason the pylons outside your window
    carry 400,000 volts.
  </p>

  <h2>Nodal and mesh analysis: the general-purpose machines</h2>
  <p>
    Series-parallel simplification eventually fails, and it is worth seeing the failure
    clearly. Add a second battery into one branch of an innocent-looking circuit and
    suddenly no two resistors are simply in series or parallel any more — with two
    sources there is no single "total resistance" to compute. A bridge network (four
    resistors in a diamond) fails the same way whenever current flows through the middle:
    every candidate pair turns out to have another path separating it. The heart of the
    difficulty is always the same: <em>more than one unknown</em>, and series-parallel
    reduction only ever solves for one. The systematic fallback is
    <strong>nodal analysis</strong>: choose a ground node, write KCL at every other node
    in terms of the unknown node voltages, and solve the simultaneous equations. With n
    nodes there are n − 1 unknowns — modest algebra for a computer.
    <strong>Mesh analysis</strong> is its mirror image: assign a circulating current to
    each independent loop, write KVL around each loop, and solve for the loop currents.
    The two methods are equivalent in power; nodal analysis wins when the circuit has few
    nodes and many loops (which is most electronics), and it is the method modern
    simulators mechanise.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example with an engineering answer at the end, borrowed from the classic
    treatment: a 5 V source feeds a 50 Ω resistor into a node, which connects through
    300 Ω to ground, through 100 Ω to a second node, which feeds a 2 Ω lamp to ground.
    No two resistors are simply in series or parallel. Choose ground, write the two KCL
    equations (one per unknown node), and solve: the first node sits at about 3.02 V and
    the lamp node at about 0.059 V, so the lamp current is 0.059 ÷ 2 ≈ 29.6 mA. Now the
    engineering part: if this lamp needs 50 mA to glow and burns out above 75 mA, the
    circuit is wrong — under-driven, needing a lower supply or smaller resistances. The
    number alone was never the point; the decision it settles is. One practical tip when
    you write the equations yourself: each node's own voltage carries a plus sign every
    time it appears in that node's equation — a quick signature for catching sign errors.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider what nodal analysis does to that bridge circuit — no simplification
    applies, yet naming the two middle nodes and writing two KCL equations solves it
    completely. The same recipe extends without modification to networks with controlled
    sources, and SPICE — the simulation engine behind most chip design since the 1970s —
    is little more than nodal analysis performed at industrial scale, stamping each
    component into a giant matrix and solving it millions of times per simulation. When
    you learn the recipe here, you are learning what the software does.
  </p>

  <h2>Thevenin and Norton: every network is one source and one resistor</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest compression in circuit theory is this: any network of sources and
    resistors, viewed from two terminals, behaves <em>exactly</em> like a single voltage
    source in series with a single resistance (Thevenin's equivalent), or equivalently a
    single current source in parallel with one resistance (Norton's). Not approximately —
    exactly, for any linear network. The Thevenin voltage is what you measure at the
    terminals with nothing connected; the Thevenin resistance is what you "see" looking
    back in, with all independent sources switched off. The two portraits are
    interchangeable: the same network, written once as a voltage source and once as a
    current source, and converting between them is one division — a source's
    open-circuit voltage divided by its internal resistance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Work the divider through it. Open-circuit output: 6 V. Looking back from the tap with
    the 9 V source replaced by a wire, R1 and R2 sit in parallel: (1000 × 2000) ÷ 3000
    ≈ 667 Ω. Now attach a 1 kΩ load. The loaded output is 6 × 1000 ÷ 1667 ≈ 3.6 V — a
    40 percent sag that the naive divider formula never predicted. Thevenin's theorem
    explained the sag in one step, and it prescribes the cure: make the divider's
    resistances small compared with the load, shrinking the Thevenin resistance, so the
    tap holds its voltage. Every real source — battery, lab supply, sensor, audio
    amplifier — is honestly specified this way, by its open-circuit voltage and internal
    resistance, and every "loading effect" puzzle in measurement dissolves into two
    resistors in series.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet the meter reading before the sag is computed" widget="predict" config={dcLoadPredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the Thevenin resistance is a physical resistor hidden
  somewhere in the circuit. It is not — it is a bookkeeping artefact, the lumped
  behaviour of the entire network as seen from outside. Open the box and you will find
  the original components untouched; close it and the two terminals genuinely cannot
  tell the difference between the full network and one 667 Ω resistor behind a 6 V
  source.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does the same current leave the battery that enters it — and what, then, does the battery actually supply?</li>
    <li>A divider has Vin = 12 V, R1 = 4 kΩ, R2 = 8 kΩ. What is the unloaded output? Now attach an 8 kΩ load: use Thevenin's method to find the new output.</li>
    <li>In the lamp circuit, nodal analysis gave 29.6 mA through a lamp that glows at 50 mA. What do you conclude, and what would you change first?</li>
    <li>Why do transmission grids raise voltage to cut losses, when P = V²/R suggests higher voltage means higher loss?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) KCL — charge is conserved, so the flow rate in equals
    the flow rate out; the battery supplies <em>energy</em>, lifting each coulomb's
    cargo of joules back up to full potential for another lap. (2) Unloaded:
    12 × 8 ÷ 12 = 8 V. Thevenin resistance: source replaced by wire, 4 kΩ and 8 kΩ in
    parallel = (4 × 8) ÷ 12 ≈ 2.67 kΩ. Loaded: 8 × 8 ÷ (8 + 2.67) = 8 × 8 ÷ 10.67 = 6 V
    — a 25 percent sag. If you instead re-solved the whole three-resistor network, you
    got the right answer the slow way — Thevenin exists to make this a thirty-second
    calculation. (3) The circuit is under-driven: no glow. First move: reduce the series
    resistances or raise the supply — Thevenin thinking again, since what the lamp sees
    is one source with one internal resistance. (4) The correct form for line losses is
    P = I²R: raising the transmission voltage lowers the current for the same delivered
    power, and losses fall with the square of current. P = V²/R applies to the
    <em>load</em>, not the line — using the wrong form of the power law for the job is
    one of the classic exam traps.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Voltage is energy per charge, current is charge per second, resistance is the friction relating them; the water analogy helps until you push it — electricity is not water.</li>
    <li>Ohm's law V = IR is a material property, not a conservation law; Kirchhoff's current law (charge) and voltage law (energy) are the two pillars.</li>
    <li>Ground is a chosen reference node; current arrows are reference directions — negative results are information, not errors.</li>
    <li>Series resistances add; parallel conductances add; but two-source and bridge networks defeat series-parallel reduction — that is what nodal and mesh analysis are for.</li>
    <li>Nodal analysis (KCL at every non-ground node) is the recipe SPICE automates at industrial scale.</li>
    <li>Any linear network at two terminals is exactly one source plus one resistance — Thevenin (series, voltage) or Norton (parallel, current); loading effects become two resistors in series.</li>
    <li>P = VI = I²R = V²/R — pick the form that matches the question; line losses use I²R, which is why grids run at 400,000 volts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson, on AC circuits and transients, keeps this skeleton and changes the
    cast: resistance generalises to impedance, complex numbers join the algebra, and the
    same Kirchhoff laws solve circuits whose voltages oscillate. After that, electronics
    introduces diodes and transistors — components that break Ohm's law on purpose, and
    whose analysis linearises around an operating point using exactly the Thevenin
    machinery built here. Digital logic and microcontrollers then show what billions of
    switches bought with those ideas, and power systems scale the P = I²R argument of
    this lesson to a continent.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two more principles round out the linear toolkit. <em>Superposition</em> says that in
    any linear circuit with several sources, each source's contribution can be computed
    alone — other sources switched off — and the answers added; it is why "noise pickup"
    and "signal" can be analysed separately and then combined, and it is the deeper
    reason Thevenin's theorem works at all. The <em>maximum power transfer theorem</em>
    says a load extracts the most power from a Thevenin source when the load resistance
    equals the Thevenin resistance, delivering 50 percent efficiency — fine for a radio
    antenna, deliberately avoided in a power grid, where matching the generator would
    waste half the plant. Note the subtlety the theorem hides: maximum <em>power</em> and
    maximum <em>voltage transfer</em> are different goals, and which one you design for
    depends on whether you are delivering energy or information.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="call the magnitude — matched-load efficiency" widget="estimate" config={dcTransferEstimate} />
  <p>
    Beyond DC, the same skeleton carries everything. Replace resistance with impedance —
    resistance generalised to capacitors and inductors, whose opposition depends on
    frequency — and Ohm's and Kirchhoff's laws survive intact with complex numbers, which
    is what phasor AC analysis is. Nonlinearity is the honest boundary: diodes and
    transistors break Ohm's law, and the working method there is to linearise around an
    operating point — Thevenin equivalents again, computed at the bias point. Where the
    research edge lives: networks too large for exact solution (power grids under
    stochastic renewables), circuits whose <em>topology</em> switches millions of times
    per second in power converters, and thermal–electrical coupling, where resistance
    itself drifts with temperature until a "linear" circuit quietly stops being one.
  </p>

